Bounded type parameters.

An unconstrained T can be any type, so inside a generic class or method, you can only call Object's methods on it — to-string, equals. And that's often too little. A bounded type parameter narrows T to "any type that is a something", which unlocks that something's methods, while still staying generic.

You write an upper bound with extends. T extends Bound means "T is Bound, or a subtype of it". Inside, T now has all of Bound's members. Take a max method: static, T extends comparable of T, returning a T, taking a list of T. It starts with the first element as best, then loops, and whenever the current element's compare-to best is greater than zero, it updates best. Now, without the bound, that compare-to call wouldn't compile, because a bare T has no compare-to. The bound is the promise that lets the compiler allow it, and it's also the requirement the caller must meet. Max of a list of three, one, two works, because integer is comparable. But max of a list of some runnable is rejected at compile time.

One point of confusion: the keyword is always extends in a bound — even when the bound is an interface. T extends comparable, T extends number, T extends closeable — all of them read as "T is a something". So a bound of number lets you call double-value or int-value on T; a bound of an interface lets you call that interface's methods. It's a constraint on the type argument, not a class-extension relationship.

A type parameter can also require several bounds at once, joined with an ampersand — at most one may be a class, and it must come first, with the rest being interfaces. Take clamp: T extends number and comparable of T. Now T must be both a number and comparable. So clamp can use compare-to, from comparable, to check against a low and a high bound, and it can also use number's methods like double-value. T satisfies both.

There's also a shape that looks odd at first: the bound can mention T itself. T extends comparable of T says "T can be compared to its own type". And that's exactly right for self-comparison. It's why it's the canonical signature for max, sort, and friends — it stops you comparing apples to oranges, because the comparable must be parameterised on T. You'll meet this shape constantly in the standard library; just recognise it as "a type comparable with itself".

Finally, where this is heading. A bound constrains the type parameter you declare — the type the method works in terms of. But it does not, by itself, make an API flexible about related types. A list-of-number parameter still won't accept a list-of-integer argument, because generics are invariant — a list of integer is not a list of number. Solving that — letting a method accept "a list of number, or any subtype" — needs a different tool: the wildcard, which is the next two sections. So, bounds say what a type can do; wildcards say how flexible a parameter's type argument may be. Together, they're what make generic APIs both safe and usable.
